1
00:00:05,358 --> 00:00:09,358
Why should I pause to ask how much of my shrinking from Provis might be traced to

2
00:00:09,358 --> 00:00:15,078
Estella? Why should I loiter on my road, to compare the state of mind in which I

3
00:00:15,078 --> 00:00:20,358
had tried to rid myself of the stain of the prison before meeting her at the coach-office,

4
00:00:20,358 --> 00:00:26,879
with the state of mind in which I now reflected on the abyss between Estella in her pride and beauty,

5
00:00:26,879 --> 00:00:32,758
and the returned transport whom I harboured? The road would be none the smoother for it,

6
00:00:32,758 --> 00:00:39,796
the end would be none the better for it, he would not be helped, nor I extenuated.

7
00:00:39,796 --> 00:00:44,796
A new fear had been engendered in my mind by his narrative; or rather,

8
00:00:44,796 --> 00:00:50,957
his narrative had given form and purpose to the fear that was already there. If Compeyson were

9
00:00:50,957 --> 00:00:57,436
alive and should discover his return, I could hardly doubt the consequence. That Compeyson

10
00:00:57,438 --> 00:01:02,519
stood in mortal fear of him, neither of the two could know much better than I;

11
00:01:02,519 --> 00:01:07,879
and that any such man as that man had been described to be would hesitate to release himself

12
00:01:07,879 --> 00:01:15,680
for good from a dreaded enemy by the safe means of becoming an informer was scarcely to be imagined.

13
00:01:15,680 --> 00:01:23,438
Never had I breathed, and never would I breathe—or so I resolved—a word of Estella to Provis. But,

14
00:01:23,438 --> 00:01:26,480
I said to Herbert that, before I could go abroad,

15
00:01:26,480 --> 00:01:33,000
I must see both Estella and Miss Havisham. This was when we were left alone on the night of the

16
00:01:33,000 --> 00:01:40,839
day when Provis told us his story. I resolved to go out to Richmond next day, and I went.

17
00:01:40,839 --> 00:01:45,917
On my presenting myself at Mrs. Brandley’s, Estella’s maid was called to tell that Estella

18
00:01:45,917 --> 00:01:53,796
had gone into the country. Where? To Satis House, as usual. Not as usual, I said, for

19
00:01:53,796 --> 00:02:00,957
she had never yet gone there without me; when was she coming back? There was an air of reservation

20
00:02:00,957 --> 00:02:06,398
in the answer which increased my perplexity, and the answer was, that her maid believed she was

21
00:02:06,398 --> 00:02:11,917
only coming back at all for a little while. I could make nothing of this, except that it was

22
00:02:11,917 --> 00:02:17,718
meant that I should make nothing of it, and I went home again in complete discomfiture.

23
00:02:18,718 --> 00:02:24,718
Another night consultation with Herbert after Provis was gone home (I always took him home,

24
00:02:24,718 --> 00:02:30,160
and always looked well about me), led us to the conclusion that nothing should be said about going

25
00:02:30,160 --> 00:02:37,199
abroad until I came back from Miss Havisham’s. In the mean time, Herbert and I were to consider

26
00:02:37,199 --> 00:02:42,199
separately what it would be best to say; whether we should devise any pretence of being afraid

27
00:02:42,199 --> 00:02:47,957
that he was under suspicious observation; or whether I, who had never yet been abroad,

28
00:02:47,957 --> 00:02:54,358
should propose an expedition. We both knew that I had but to propose anything, and he would

29
00:02:54,358 --> 00:03:01,436
consent. We agreed that his remaining many days in his present hazard was not to be thought of.

30
00:03:01,438 --> 00:03:06,839
Next day I had the meanness to feign that I was under a binding promise to go down to Joe;

31
00:03:06,839 --> 00:03:12,839
but I was capable of almost any meanness towards Joe or his name. Provis was to be

32
00:03:12,839 --> 00:03:17,319
strictly careful while I was gone, and Herbert was to take the charge of him

33
00:03:17,319 --> 00:03:23,199
that I had taken. I was to be absent only one night, and, on my return,

34
00:03:23,199 --> 00:03:27,758
the gratification of his impatience for my starting as a gentleman on a greater scale

35
00:03:27,758 --> 00:03:34,559
was to be begun. It occurred to me then, and as I afterwards found to Herbert also, that he

36
00:03:34,559 --> 00:03:41,358
might be best got away across the water, on that pretence,—as, to make purchases, or the like.

37
00:03:41,358 --> 00:03:46,078
Having thus cleared the way for my expedition to Miss Havisham’s, I set off by the early morning

38
00:03:46,078 --> 00:03:51,758
coach before it was yet light, and was out on the open country road when the day came creeping on,

39
00:03:51,758 --> 00:03:57,038
halting and whimpering and shivering, and wrapped in patches of cloud and rags of mist,

40
00:03:57,038 --> 00:04:03,480
like a beggar. When we drove up to the Blue Boar after a drizzly ride, whom should I see come out

41
00:04:03,480 --> 00:04:09,480
under the gateway, toothpick in hand, to look at the coach, but Bentley Drummle!

42
00:04:09,480 --> 00:04:15,358
As he pretended not to see me, I pretended not to see him. It was a very lame pretence

43
00:04:15,358 --> 00:04:19,917
on both sides; the lamer, because we both went into the coffee-room,

44
00:04:19,917 --> 00:04:25,519
where he had just finished his breakfast, and where I ordered mine. It was poisonous

45
00:04:25,519 --> 00:04:31,277
to me to see him in the town, for I very well knew why he had come there.

46
00:04:31,277 --> 00:04:36,519
Pretending to read a smeary newspaper long out of date, which had nothing half so legible in

47
00:04:36,519 --> 00:04:42,160
its local news, as the foreign matter of coffee, pickles, fish sauces, gravy,

48
00:04:42,160 --> 00:04:46,718
melted butter, and wine with which it was sprinkled all over, as if it had taken the

49
00:04:46,718 --> 00:04:54,237
measles in a highly irregular form, I sat at my table while he stood before the fire. By

50
00:04:54,237 --> 00:05:00,678
degrees it became an enormous injury to me that he stood before the fire. And I got up,

51
00:05:00,680 --> 00:05:06,116
determined to have my share of it. I had to put my hand behind his legs for the poker

52
00:05:06,116 --> 00:05:12,116
when I went up to the fireplace to stir the fire, but still pretended not to know him.

53
00:05:12,116 --> 00:05:15,160
“Is this a cut?” said Mr. Drummle.

54
00:05:15,160 --> 00:05:17,917
“Oh!” said I, poker in hand;

55
00:05:17,917 --> 00:05:25,000
“it’s you, is it? How do you do? I was wondering who it was, who kept the fire off.”

56
00:05:25,000 --> 00:05:31,438
With that, I poked tremendously, and having done so, planted myself side by side with Mr. Drummle,

57
00:05:31,438 --> 00:05:34,917
my shoulders squared and my back to the fire.

58
00:05:34,917 --> 00:05:41,358
“You have just come down?” said Mr. Drummle, edging me a little away with his shoulder.

59
00:05:41,358 --> 00:05:45,436
“Yes,” said I, edging him a little away with my shoulder.

60
00:05:45,438 --> 00:05:50,398
“Beastly place,” said Drummle. “Your part of the country, I think?”

61
00:05:50,398 --> 00:05:54,877
“Yes,” I assented. “I am told it’s very like your Shropshire.”

62
00:05:54,879 --> 00:05:58,000
“Not in the least like it,” said Drummle.

63
00:05:58,000 --> 00:06:01,116
Here Mr. Drummle looked at his boots and I looked at mine,

64
00:06:01,116 --> 00:06:05,319
and then Mr. Drummle looked at my boots, and I looked at his.

65
00:06:05,319 --> 00:06:11,480
“Have you been here long?” I asked, determined not to yield an inch of the fire.

66
00:06:11,480 --> 00:06:17,519
“Long enough to be tired of it,” returned Drummle, pretending to yawn, but equally determined.

67
00:06:17,519 --> 00:06:19,597
“Do you stay here long?”

68
00:06:19,597 --> 00:06:24,358
“Can’t say,” answered Mr. Drummle. “Do you?”

69
00:06:24,358 --> 00:06:27,000
“Can’t say,” said I.

70
00:06:27,000 --> 00:06:32,160
I felt here, through a tingling in my blood, that if Mr. Drummle’s shoulder had claimed

71
00:06:32,160 --> 00:06:37,796
another hair’s breadth of room, I should have jerked him into the window; equally,

72
00:06:37,796 --> 00:06:40,796
that if my own shoulder had urged a similar claim,

73
00:06:40,796 --> 00:06:47,519
Mr. Drummle would have jerked me into the nearest box. He whistled a little. So did I.

74
00:06:47,519 --> 00:06:51,718
“Large tract of marshes about here, I believe?” said Drummle.

75
00:06:51,718 --> 00:06:54,199
“Yes. What of that?” said I.

76
00:06:54,199 --> 00:06:59,597
Mr. Drummle looked at me, and then at my boots, and then said, “Oh!” and laughed.

77
00:06:59,597 --> 00:07:02,160
“Are you amused, Mr. Drummle?”

78
00:07:02,160 --> 00:07:06,436
“No,” said he, “not particularly. I am going out for a ride in the saddle. I

79
00:07:06,438 --> 00:07:09,480
mean to explore those marshes for amusement. Out-of-the-way

80
00:07:09,480 --> 00:07:16,398
villages there, they tell me. Curious little public-houses—and smithies—and that. Waiter!”

81
00:07:16,398 --> 00:07:17,480
“Yes, sir.”

82
00:07:17,480 --> 00:07:19,639
“Is that horse of mine ready?”

83
00:07:19,641 --> 00:07:21,796
“Brought round to the door, sir.”

84
00:07:21,796 --> 00:07:27,078
“I say. Look here, you sir. The lady won’t ride to-day; the weather won’t do.”

85
00:07:27,078 --> 00:07:28,680
“Very good, sir.”

86
00:07:28,680 --> 00:07:33,078
“And I don’t dine, because I’m going to dine at the lady’s.”

87
00:07:33,078 --> 00:07:34,758
“Very good, sir.”

88
00:07:34,758 --> 00:07:40,839
Then, Drummle glanced at me, with an insolent triumph on his great-jowled face that cut me

89
00:07:40,839 --> 00:07:47,360
to the heart, dull as he was, and so exasperated me, that I felt inclined to take him in my arms

90
00:07:48,000 --> 00:07:54,160
(as the robber in the story-book is said to have taken the old lady) and seat him on the fire.

91
00:07:54,160 --> 00:07:59,277
One thing was manifest to both of us, and that was, that until relief came,

92
00:07:59,277 --> 00:08:04,879
neither of us could relinquish the fire. There we stood, well squared up before it,

93
00:08:04,879 --> 00:08:11,237
shoulder to shoulder and foot to foot, with our hands behind us, not budging an inch. The

94
00:08:11,237 --> 00:08:16,237
horse was visible outside in the drizzle at the door, my breakfast was put on the table,

95
00:08:16,237 --> 00:08:23,078
Drummle’s was cleared away, the waiter invited me to begin, I nodded, we both stood our ground.

96
00:08:23,078 --> 00:08:26,116
“Have you been to the Grove since?” said Drummle.

97
00:08:26,116 --> 00:08:31,000
“No,” said I, “I had quite enough of the Finches the last time I was there.”

98
00:08:31,000 --> 00:08:33,917
“Was that when we had a difference of opinion?”

99
00:08:33,917 --> 00:08:36,116
“Yes,” I replied, very shortly.

100
00:08:36,116 --> 00:08:39,116
“Come, come! They let you off easily enough,” sneered

101
00:08:39,116 --> 00:08:42,116
Drummle. “You shouldn’t have lost your temper.”

102
00:08:42,116 --> 00:08:47,398
“Mr. Drummle,” said I, “you are not competent to give advice on that subject. When I lose

103
00:08:47,398 --> 00:08:52,639
my temper (not that I admit having done so on that occasion), I don’t throw glasses.”

104
00:08:52,641 --> 00:08:54,758
“I do,” said Drummle.

105
00:08:54,758 --> 00:09:01,000
After glancing at him once or twice, in an increased state of smouldering ferocity, I said,—

106
00:09:01,000 --> 00:09:05,839
“Mr. Drummle, I did not seek this conversation, and I don’t think it an agreeable one.”

107
00:09:05,839 --> 00:09:12,277
“I am sure it’s not,” said he, superciliously over his shoulder; “I don’t think anything about it.”

108
00:09:12,277 --> 00:09:15,480
“And therefore,” I went on, “with your leave,

109
00:09:15,480 --> 00:09:20,277
I will suggest that we hold no kind of communication in future.”

110
00:09:20,277 --> 00:09:25,957
“Quite my opinion,” said Drummle, “and what I should have suggested myself, or done—more

111
00:09:25,957 --> 00:09:33,398
likely—without suggesting. But don’t lose your temper. Haven’t you lost enough without that?”

112
00:09:33,398 --> 00:09:35,038
“What do you mean, sir?”

113
00:09:35,038 --> 00:09:38,398
“Waiter!” said Drummle, by way of answering me.

114
00:09:38,398 --> 00:09:40,078
The waiter reappeared.

115
00:09:40,078 --> 00:09:45,277
“Look here, you sir. You quite understand that the young lady don’t ride to-day,

116
00:09:45,277 --> 00:09:47,237
and that I dine at the young lady’s?”

117
00:09:47,879 --> 00:09:49,641
“Quite so, sir!”

118
00:09:49,641 --> 00:09:54,199
When the waiter had felt my fast-cooling teapot with the palm of his hand, and

119
00:09:54,199 --> 00:10:00,957
had looked imploringly at me, and had gone out, Drummle, careful not to move the shoulder next me,

120
00:10:00,957 --> 00:10:08,237
took a cigar from his pocket and bit the end off, but showed no sign of stirring. Choking and

121
00:10:08,237 --> 00:10:15,116
boiling as I was, I felt that we could not go a word further, without introducing Estella’s name,

122
00:10:15,116 --> 00:10:21,277
which I could not endure to hear him utter; and therefore I looked stonily at the opposite wall,

123
00:10:21,277 --> 00:10:27,957
as if there were no one present, and forced myself to silence. How long we might have remained in

124
00:10:27,957 --> 00:10:34,519
this ridiculous position it is impossible to say, but for the incursion of three thriving

125
00:10:34,519 --> 00:10:40,277
farmers—laid on by the waiter, I think—who came into the coffee-room unbuttoning their great-coats

126
00:10:40,277 --> 00:10:47,557
and rubbing their hands, and before whom, as they charged at the fire, we were obliged to give way.

127
00:10:47,559 --> 00:10:52,957
I saw him through the window, seizing his horse’s mane, and mounting in his blundering brutal

128
00:10:52,957 --> 00:10:59,680
manner, and sidling and backing away. I thought he was gone, when he came back, calling for a

129
00:10:59,680 --> 00:11:05,917
light for the cigar in his mouth, which he had forgotten. A man in a dust-coloured dress appeared

130
00:11:05,917 --> 00:11:12,000
with what was wanted,—I could not have said from where: whether from the inn yard, or the street,

131
00:11:12,000 --> 00:11:17,116
or where not,—and as Drummle leaned down from the saddle and lighted his cigar and laughed,

132
00:11:17,957 --> 00:11:21,879
with a jerk of his head towards the coffee-room windows, the slouching

133
00:11:21,879 --> 00:11:27,879
shoulders and ragged hair of this man whose back was towards me reminded me of Orlick.

134
00:11:27,879 --> 00:11:33,160
Too heavily out of sorts to care much at the time whether it were he or no, or after all

135
00:11:33,160 --> 00:11:39,199
to touch the breakfast, I washed the weather and the journey from my face and hands, and went out

136
00:11:39,199 --> 00:11:44,000
to the memorable old house that it would have been so much the better for me never to have entered,

137
00:11:44,000 --> 00:11:46,000
never to have seen. Chapter 44.
